  "response_text": "\u201cSetting up a thread\u2019s stack\u201d simply means to prepare a stack identical to what the CPU is expecting, giving it the illusion that the thread was always running, even if this is not necessarily true. To do this, we must manually push register values onto our thread\u2019s stack in a very specific order. This includes registers like the xPSR, program counter, and general-purpose registers r0-r12. Not only are these registers required in our thread\u2019s stack so that the CPU can run as expected, but it also must simply continue storing temporary data like variables, addresses, etc. for our programs to run.\\n",
